  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1123 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThe upper level jet stream dips south into the Plains this weekend. \nBy Saturday, the jet maxima is forecast to stretch across the \ncombined Panhandles. A response will be felt at the surface as these \nstrong upper level winds mix down and transition into strong \nsurface winds rotating around a surface low passing across the \nCWA. As the upper trough continues moving south, the surface low \nwill follow and a cold front will pass through the area. Surface \nwinds behind the front are still forecast to be strong during the \novernight period, but they will subside later on Sunday as a \nsurface high is reestablished over the region. For the rest of the\nweek, a series of upper level systems will move across the CONUS.\nWhile the specific tracks of these systems still have some \nuncertainty, we may generally be subject to temperature \nfluctuation as we head into December. Moisture looks to largely \nremain absent for the next 7 days as well.\n\nHighs on Saturday could approach the 70s for some areas due to some \nstronger WAA throughout the day. The NBM 24 hr maximum wind gust \nforecast for Saturday suggest there's up to a 60% chances for gusts \nto exceed 50 mph in our northwest zones while the rest of the CWA \nhas chances ranging from 10-40%. Strong northerly winds continue \ninto Sunday, which will help tank temperatures during the day. Highs \nmay not escape the 40s Sunday afternoon, but surface winds will \nfinally fall below 10 mph by the early afternoon. A warming trend\nis forecast follow for the first week of December until our next \ncold front arrives. \n\nRangel",
